 overview of these topics for clinical application and general knowledge.

10
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:45,680
 So first we're going to go over the biology of the dental pulp, because much of endodontics

11
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:52,680
 is focused on pulp-able health, since endodontics literally translates to the knowledge of what's

12
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:55,280
 within teeth.

13
00:00:55,280 --> 00:01:00,020
 So the pulp is obviously very important.

14
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:05,560
 So the pulp is the innermost part of the tooth is generally very soft and vascular.

15
00:01:05,560 --> 00:01:10,440
 So let's talk about what specifically is contained within this tissue.

16
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:16,600
 So the pulp contains loose fibrous connective tissue with nerves, blood vessels, and lymphatics.

17
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:18,560
 Again, very vascular.

18
00:01:18,560 --> 00:01:25,320
 It contains fibroblasts, which secrete fibrous connective tissue.

19
00:01:25,320 --> 00:01:31,640
 It also houses the odontoblast, which secrete dentin.

20
00:01:31,640 --> 00:01:35,680
 Now the type of dentin depends on the stage of root formation.

21
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:42,560
 So it's considered primary dentin before root formation is complete and secondary dentin

22
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:44,840
 after root formation is complete.

23
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:50,120
 But odontoblast secrete both types of dentin.

24
00:01:50,120 --> 00:01:56,840
 It also contains undifferentiated mesenchymal cells, which can differentiate into a specific

25
00:01:56,840 --> 00:02:03,400
 type of cell, which we'll revisit later called secondary odontoblast.

26
00:02:03,400 --> 00:02:09,600
 So the confusing thing here is that secondary odontoblast do not secrete secondary dentin.

27
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:15,760
 They actually form tertiary dentin to protect the pulp from injury.

28
00:02:15,760 --> 00:02:22,200
 But these undifferentiated mesenchymal cells are basically stem cells that can later on

29
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:27,880
 divide and become new cells.

30
00:02:27,880 --> 00:02:31,760
 So the pulp is also surrounded by hard dentin.

31
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:39,200
 So that's what those odontoblast have secreted as the tooth was forming and after it has completed

32
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:40,440
 information.

33
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:46,760
 And so this hard dentin creates a pressure system which limits its ability to expand.

34
00:02:46,760 --> 00:02:53,320
 So the pulp, if pressure is building up and some infection has taken place, it has a

35
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:58,280
 lot of trouble expanding against this hard dentin.

36
00:02:58,280 --> 00:03:05,640
 Also it lacks collateral circulation, which limits its ability to cope with infection.

37
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:11,720
 So basically there's one one way in, one way out for a two rooted tooth, I guess it has

38
00:03:11,720 --> 00:03:13,880
 two ways in and two ways out.

39
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:18,680
 But there are less avenues for immune cells to reach the pulp.

40
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:25,000
 So the pulp tissue as compared to say the skin of your face is already compromised structurally

41
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,800
 in terms of being able to fight an infection.

42
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:34,800
 One for the pressure buildup and two for less avenues for immune cells to reach that component.

43
00:03:34,800 --> 00:03:44,440
 So the pulp is already anatomically more driven to infection, at least more easily.

44
00:03:44,440 --> 00:03:47,960
 So let's talk about that, the dentin and pulp defense.

45
00:03:47,960 --> 00:03:55,640
 What can the pulp do rather to fight off or defend itself from an infection?

46
00:03:55,640 --> 00:04:00,600
 So sclerotic dentin is basically very hard dentin.

47
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:07,640
 The calcification has occurred of dentinal tubules in response to slowly advancing carries or

48
00:04:07,640 --> 00:04:14,640
 just aging, just the physiological process of this dentin getting harder over time.

49
00:04:14,640 --> 00:04:22,000
 So sclerotic dentin would be a pulp or response to slowly advancing carries.

50
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:29,120
 Now we have this thing called reactionary dentin, which is a reaction to minor damage.

51
00:04:29,120 --> 00:04:32,920
 So reactionary reaction to minor damage.

52
00:04:32,920 --> 00:04:45,760
 And some sources would call this another word for secondary dentin.

53
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:51,120
 Now reparative dentin is repair for major damage.

54
00:04:51,120 --> 00:04:58,560
 And some textbooks and some sources will refer to this otherwise as the tertiary dentin.

55
00:04:58,560 --> 00:05:04,180
 And this makes sense because minor damage wouldn't be enough to destroy the original

56
00:05:04,180 --> 00:05:11,840
 Adanta blasts that are pleasant, so it allows them to lay down some dentin.

57
00:05:11,840 --> 00:05:18,000
 While major damage would destroy the original Adanta blasts, and so then those undifferentiated

58
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:25,040
 mesenchymal cells need to step up and become secondary Adanta blasts and lay down tertiary

59
00:05:25,040 --> 00:05:30,080
 dentin as basically a last resort.

60
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:39,160
 However, many sources just refer to both of these together as tertiary dentin.

61
00:05:39,160 --> 00:05:43,880
 But I wouldn't worry too much about these details, it's not too, too important.

62
00:05:43,880 --> 00:05:50,200
 More important is to know that reactionary is for minor damage, whereas reparative is

63
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:51,480
 for major damage.

64
00:05:51,480 --> 00:05:57,680
 That's much, much more important to know than all the secondary and tertiary stuff.

65
00:05:57,680 --> 00:06:02,920
 So in endodontics and operative dentistry, there's this technique referred to as pulp

66
00:06:02,920 --> 00:06:10,560
 capping where you place a calcium hydroxide liner, which irritates Adanta blasts, and

67
00:06:10,560 --> 00:06:16,720
 they'll form either this reactionary or reparative dentin depending on how close you are to the

68
00:06:16,720 --> 00:06:17,720
 pulp.

69
00:06:17,720 --> 00:06:25,560
 So this sort of dynamic response of odontoblasts and these secondary Adanta blasts who lay down

70
00:06:25,560 --> 00:06:32,600
 new dentin sort of to form this dentinal wall that forms a barrier and defends the pulp.

71
00:06:32,600 --> 00:06:40,180
 So the tooth is very alive, as long as it's not necrotic, and it can respond dynamically

72
00:06:40,180 --> 00:06:43,320
 to infection, which is pretty cool.

73
00:06:43,320 --> 00:06:50,600
 And now of course we have popyl necrosis where the tooth is dead, the pulp has been compromised,

74
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:55,920
 and that's the response to rapidly advancing carries or other severe damage.

75
00:06:55,920 --> 00:07:01,400
 And the tooth has lost the battle, and the pulp is now dead.

76
00:07:01,400 --> 00:07:09,400
 So in all of these cases, the main cause of serious pulpal injury is always bacteria.

77
00:07:09,400 --> 00:07:16,900
 So bacteria can come from a plethora of sources mostly from let's think dental carries or

78
00:07:16,900 --> 00:07:22,960
 cavities, and the bacteria are small enough where they can penetrate beyond the obvious

79
00:07:22,960 --> 00:07:28,920
 carries and cavities through dentinal tubules to reach the pulp, and that's when problems

80
00:07:28,920 --> 00:07:34,400
 can start occurring, the patient can be in pain, and the pulp can become infected and

81
00:07:34,400 --> 00:07:36,040
 can die eventually.

82
00:07:36,040 --> 00:07:43,840
 Alright, so now let's talk about more specifics of the histology of the dental pulp.

83
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:50,240
 So from outside to inside, we have, well first we have dentin, that's the darkest layer right

84
00:07:50,240 --> 00:07:56,320
 here, and then the pre dentin is the innermost portion of dentin, that's the lighter portion

85
00:07:56,320 --> 00:08:02,680
 here, and it's lighter because it's not mineralized, and it's located directly adjacent to what

86
00:08:02,680 --> 00:08:07,040
 we would consider the actual pulp.

87
00:08:07,040 --> 00:08:13,720
 So the odontoblastic layer is where all of these nuclei are, these are the odontoblasts

88
00:08:13,720 --> 00:08:19,040
 that are laying down dentin right on the outside, just adjacent to the pulp tissue.

89
00:08:19,040 --> 00:08:26,880
 So the odontoblasts again are actually part of the pulp, but they are forming the dentin

90
00:08:26,880 --> 00:08:29,280
 just outside of it.

91
00:08:29,280 --> 00:08:36,560
 Now right inside the odontoblastic layer is the cell free zone of whale, and that's

92
00:08:36,560 --> 00:08:41,740
 this zone right here where there are almost zero nuclei present, and that's because there

93
00:08:41,740 --> 00:08:44,000
 are no cells there.

94
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:49,920
 In this region, you'll often see nerve bundles, and we'll talk more about nerves in the next

95
00:08:49,920 --> 00:08:52,200
 coming slides.

96
00:08:52,200 --> 00:08:56,600
 And then right inside of that is the cell rich zone, and that's where we have a lot

97
00:08:56,600 --> 00:09:03,880
 more nuclei, and then inside of that would be the pulp core, the central part of the pulp,

98
00:09:03,880 --> 00:09:08,960
 and we will talk more about that as well when we are referring to nerves.

99
00:09:08,960 --> 00:09:18,980
 Okay, so we have this thing called dentinal pain, and so this is conducted by these A

100
00:09:18,980 --> 00:09:20,880
 delta fibers.

101
00:09:20,880 --> 00:09:25,120
 So that's probably the first thing we've talked about so far that's extremely important,

102
00:09:25,120 --> 00:09:28,880
 and I would definitely, definitely know that for the exam.

103
00:09:28,880 --> 00:09:36,520
 This one, the A delta fiber is large, it's myelinated, and it's an afferent nerve, which

104
00:09:36,520 --> 00:09:42,640
 means, or afferent nerve, that means it's carrying nervous information from outside the

105
00:09:42,640 --> 00:09:48,480
 body, it's carrying it peripherally towards the center, so it's bringing information into

106
00:09:48,480 --> 00:09:50,720
 the body.

107
00:09:50,720 --> 00:09:57,680
 And so it's, as you can see in this picture, it's large, it's at least larger than this

108
00:09:57,680 --> 00:10:01,680
 one that we're going to talk about in the next slide, it's myelinated, that's what these

109
00:10:01,680 --> 00:10:08,880
 darker red portions are, and the nodes of Ranvier between these myelinated sheets, and

110
00:10:08,880 --> 00:10:11,600
 it's afferent.

111
00:10:11,600 --> 00:10:19,080
 So the dentinal pain that's conducted by these A delta fibers is a sharp transient first

112
00:10:19,080 --> 00:10:25,760
 pain, so if you stub your toe, that's the initial really sharp pain that you would feel

113
00:10:25,760 --> 00:10:27,140
 from that.

114
00:10:27,140 --> 00:10:34,520
 These fibers course coronaly through the pulp, so they're coronaly, as opposed to, say, centrally,

115
00:10:34,520 --> 00:10:40,640
 which is where the C fibers are going to be, and dentinal pain is more often than not associated

116
00:10:40,640 --> 00:10:48,200
 with cold temperatures.

117
00:10:48,200 --> 00:10:54,560
 And now the second type of pain is pulpitus pain, and that's conducted by these C fibers,

118
00:10:54,560 --> 00:10:59,840
 so when we compare them to the A delta fibers, they're small, they're unmyelinated, and they're

119
00:10:59,840 --> 00:11:03,840
 also afferent nerves, they're carrying this pain information.

120
00:11:03,840 --> 00:11:08,840
 As I said before, they course centrally in the pulp stroma.

121
00:11:08,840 --> 00:11:16,520
 This one is involved with dull, throbbing second pain, so after you stub your toe, and it hurts

122
00:11:16,520 --> 00:11:22,160
 a lot initially, and then it kind of is sore and achy a little bit later, that would be

123
00:11:22,160 --> 00:11:27,600
 the second pain, and this is more often associated with heat.

124
00:11:27,600 --> 00:11:34,400
 So now that we talked about both of these, you can see this chart here, or this graph,

125
00:11:34,400 --> 00:11:44,240
 with time on the x-axis, and the A delta axons are transmitting this first pain, super painful,

126
00:11:44,240 --> 00:11:49,280
 and then the second pain sort of creeps up later, and it's a lot longer, maybe not quite

127
00:11:49,280 --> 00:11:53,600
 as intense, but it's managed a lot later.

128
00:11:53,600 --> 00:11:58,720
 So those are the two different types of pain, and the two different types of fibers associated

129
00:11:58,720 --> 00:12:00,960
 with each of those pains.

130
00:12:00,960 --> 00:12:06,440
 Now I'll go back one slide real quick, because I wanted to mention that the A delta fibers

131
00:12:06,440 --> 00:12:15,400
 are transmitting dentinal pain, because they are associated with the pulpo-dentinal complex,

132
00:12:15,400 --> 00:12:22,120
 and so that means where the pulp and the dentin meet, it's a bit more easily provoked, because

133
00:12:22,120 --> 00:12:29,520
 dentinal pain is further on the outside, you can say, and because the fibers aren't central

134
00:12:29,520 --> 00:12:34,040
 to the pulp, they're more coronal, they're more on the outside, along the outside border,

135
00:12:34,040 --> 00:12:41,080
 they're more easily provoked than the C fibers, which are located more central.

136
00:12:41,080 --> 00:12:47,720
 And so you can think of it, the progression of pulpl inflammation can change a pain response

137
00:12:47,720 --> 00:12:58,540
 from this first pain with A delta axons to become second pain transmitted by the C axons.

138
00:12:58,540 --> 00:13:06,360
 All right, next I want to talk about these two really important terms for pain sensitization,

139
00:13:06,360 --> 00:13:09,960
 and there are some graphs and drawings you can check out for these terms, but I actually

140
00:13:09,960 --> 00:13:14,040
 thought they were very confusing, so I'll try to explain these important terms as best

141
00:13:14,040 --> 00:13:15,760
 as I can.

142
00:13:15,760 --> 00:13:23,280
 First we have hyperalgezia, which is a heightened response to pain, and then that's compared

143
00:13:23,280 --> 00:13:31,800
 to allodynia, which is a reduced pain threshold, so that's pain due to a stimulus that does

144
00:13:31,800 --> 00:13:36,560
 not normally provoke pain.

145
00:13:36,560 --> 00:13:44,840
 So hyperalgezia would be where you have inflammatory mediators in the pulp that can increase the

146
00:13:44,840 --> 00:13:51,080
 intensity of a pain stimulus, in other words, something that's usually painful becomes even

147
00:13:51,080 --> 00:13:58,640
 more painful, and for allodynia, I have a great way to remember this, and a great example

148
00:13:58,640 --> 00:14:02,640
 of allodynia is sunburn.

149
00:14:02,640 --> 00:14:09,680
 So usually touching your skin doesn't hurt, but when it's badly sunburned, it physiologically

150
00:14:09,680 --> 00:14:17,640
 hurts when you touch your skin, and alloe is something you often use to treat symptoms

151
00:14:17,640 --> 00:14:25,380
 of sunburn, so I think of it like sunburned skin is an example of allodynia, and alloe

152
00:14:25,380 --> 00:14:30,920
 being, again, the thing that you would often grab when you're experiencing these terrible

153
00:14:30,920 --> 00:14:36,860
 symptoms of a bad sunburn, so hopefully that can help you differentiate and remember between

154
00:14:36,860 --> 00:14:42,320
 these two important pain sensitization terms.

155
00:14:42,320 --> 00:14:47,560
 And last, I just wanted to review the concept of referred pain, which is important in all

156
00:14:47,560 --> 00:14:52,120
 of dentistry, and particularly in the dontics.

157
00:14:52,120 --> 00:15:00,400
 So pre-arricular pain often refers from mandibular molars since both share V3 innervation.

158
00:15:00,400 --> 00:15:04,540
 Okay, so what does this all mean, and why is it important for anodontics?

159
00:15:04,540 --> 00:15:11,440
 So pre-arricular is referring to the region in front of the ear, and you'd think pain

160
00:15:11,440 --> 00:15:19,080
 in front of the ears may be referred from, say, maxillary molars because, well, they're

161
00:15:19,080 --> 00:15:24,800
 anatomically much closer, but with how the innervation of the face works, as we can see

162
00:15:24,800 --> 00:15:35,160
 in this diagram, although they may be anatomically closer, they share, or this area in front of

163
00:15:35,160 --> 00:15:41,520
 the ear shares V3 innervation with the mandibular molars.

164
00:15:41,520 --> 00:15:48,720
 Since V3 is the mandibular nerve, that is what shares innervation with this part of

165
00:15:48,720 --> 00:15:49,720
 the ear.

